  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+3v-5,\\,y=2v+u-4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving",
  "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v-5 \\\\ 2v+u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
